// SOS M3 unit 2.75: A DEAD PROCESS'S SLOT COMES BACK WHEN THE LAST HANDLE ONTO
// IT DOES (sawos design 3 D-3), closing the pend unit 2 recorded at
// `alloc_process`.
//
// The transcript is read as an ORDER, and the two flags are the two sides of one
// claim:
//
//   SOS reclaim: started
//   SOS: process fault: bad handle process=1     <- the child, dying
//   SOS: process teardown ... process=1
//   SOS reclaim: refused_before=1 reclaimed_after=1
//   SOS reclaim: done
//
// **`refused_before=1` IS THE HALF THAT IS EASY TO LOSE.** With the child dead
// but root still holding its Process handle, a create with the table otherwise
// full must be refused — the dead slot is `Gone`, not `Free`, because the §8
// status word in it still has a possible reader. That refusal is the behaviour
// unit 2 shipped, and asserting it here is what stops this case from passing on
// a kernel that simply reclaimed slots eagerly and lost the supervision story.
//
// **"THE TABLE OTHERWISE FULL" IS NOW A THING THIS CASE HAS TO ARRANGE**
// (sawos design 21, M4 unit 5): `MAX_PROCESSES` is THREE since driver-as-
// service, so root plus a dead child leaves a slot free and the refusal would
// simply not happen. The case creates one unstarted FILLER process to spend it.
// That is the shape every `MAX_PROCESSES` bump will want here, and it is why
// the proof is written against "the table is full" rather than against a
// number.
//
// **`reclaimed_after=1` IS THE UNIT.** Root drops the child's handle — no method
// call, just a value going out of scope, which is what `NoCopy` + `deinit` makes
// a release (design 3 D-5) — and the create that was refused a moment ago now
// succeeds. Between the two calls the only thing that changed is that no handle
// anywhere names slot 1 any more, which is precisely the reader-count the
// reclaim asks for.
//
// WHY A READER-COUNT AT ALL, since it reads like bookkeeping: the only thing a
// `Gone` slot still holds is its §8 status word, and the only way to read that
// word is `GetStatus` through a Process handle. So "no handle names the slot"
// IS "no possible reader", exactly — which is why this slab reclaims and no
// other does. Events, Waiters, Timers and Memories still come back only at their
// process's teardown, because their "may I free this" question needs a refcount
// nothing yet justifies.
//
// D-1's GENERATIONS ARE WHAT MAKE THE REUSE SAFE. The second child lands in the
// slot the first one had; a word onto the first child would now name a live slot
// at the wrong life and resolve to nothing. Nothing in this case can hold such a
// word — the typed layer has no word extractor, by design — so the stale-word
// half of the claim is `handle-remint`'s, made where words are visible.
//
// ROOT PARKS ON A GENEROUS TIMER while the child runs, so the ordering above is
// forced rather than raced: this image's kernel arms no scheduler tick, so the
// only way root gives the processor up is the wait, and the only way it gets it
// back is the timer it armed.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, BootHandle, Clock, ClockType, Timer,
            Waiter, SosStatus}

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

static YES: UInt = 1
static NO: UInt = 0

/// The key this case attaches its timer under.
static KEY: UInt = 88

/// Twenty milliseconds — three orders of magnitude more than the child needs to
/// be scheduled, run its one syscall and die. See `process-lifecycle`.
static PARK_NS: UInt64 = 20_000_000

func flag(b: Bool) -> UInt {
    if b { YES } else { NO }
}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS reclaim: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // Row 0 is the child's image blob, row 1 the RAM it runs in — this build's
    // config, which is root's business and never the kernel's.
    var image = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: no image region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var dest = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: no destination region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // The record hands the region over — see `process-lifecycle` for why
    // `take_memory` is the one way out (sawos design 3 D-5). BOTH regions are
    // used TWICE here, which is the reason they are taken into locals: the
    // second child is loaded from the same image into the same RAM, the first
    // child's remains being nothing the kernel tracks.
    let image_mem = image.take_memory()!
    let dest_mem = dest.take_memory()!

    let child = try system.process_create(image: &image_mem,
                                          memory: &dest_mem) catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: create failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try child.start() catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: start failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS reclaim: started\n")

    // Park, so the child gets the processor and dies before anything below runs.
    let clock = try system.clock_get(type: ClockType.Monotonic) catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: no clock: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let waiter = try proc.waiter_create() catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: no waiter: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let timer = try clock.timer_create() catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: no timer: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = waiter.add(&timer, KEY)
    let _ = timer.arm(after_ns: PARK_NS)
    try waiter.wait() catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: wait failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // **THE FILLER, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE M4 UNIT 5 RAISED `MAX_PROCESSES` TO
    // THREE** (sawos design 21, user-ruled Sep 2). This case's claim is about a
    // `Gone` slot being unavailable while a reader can still name it, and that
    // claim is only observable when the table has NOTHING ELSE to give — so the
    // proof needs as many live processes as the bump added. One create, never
    // started, occupying the slot the third process would otherwise take.
    //
    // It is the same image into the same RAM as the child that has already
    // died, which is the reason those two regions were taken into locals in the
    // first place: the first child's remains are nothing the kernel tracks.
    // Nothing runs in this process — a created-and-unstarted slot holds a
    // domain and no thread, which is exactly the resource this case is about.
    let filler = try system.process_create(image: &image_mem,
                                           memory: &dest_mem) catch {
        print("SOS reclaim: filler create failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // BEFORE THE RELEASE: the slot is `Gone`, not `Free`, because root's handle
    // onto it keeps the §8 status word readable. With root, the filler and the
    // dead child's slot there is nowhere for this create to go, so it is refused
    // with a STATUS — the caller could not have known the table was full, which
    // is what keeps this out of the faults ruling.
    let refused_before = match system.process_create(image: &image_mem,
                                                     memory: &dest_mem) {
        case Ok(_) -> false,
        case Err(_) -> true,
    }

    // THE RELEASE, WRITTEN AS A DROP. `let _ = move child` consumes the wrapper
    // and its `deinit` issues the release — there is no typed `release()` method
    // to call, because dropping IS the spelling (design 3 D-5). With that entry
    // unbound, no handle in any process's table names slot 1, so the kernel's
    // reclaim scan frees it.
    let _ = move child

    let reclaimed_after = match system.process_create(image: &image_mem,
                                                      memory: &dest_mem) {
        case Ok(_) -> true,
        case Err(_) -> false,
    }

    print("SOS reclaim: refused_before={} reclaimed_after={}\n",
          flag(refused_before), flag(reclaimed_after))
    // **THE FILLER'S HANDLE IS KEPT TO THE END ON PURPOSE.** Releasing it would
    // leave a `Created` slot nothing names — and `reclaim_process_slot` frees
    // only `Gone` slots, because a slot that never ran has no §8 status word to
    // decide readability by. Holding the handle is what keeps this case's own
    // arithmetic honest and does not provoke a state no unit has ruled on.
    print("SOS reclaim: done\n")
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
}
